Found by a coordinator feature-gap review, 2026-08-30. Depends on B035.

## Gap
Five diagnostic families are mechanically fixable but offer no edit. Unlike
B036 these need a little type knowledge, not just a span replacement.

- `FloatTruncation { literal: bool }` (checker.rs) — two message variants:
  `literal: true` (`int ms = 3.7;`) and `literal: false` (`int g = f;`).
  Fixes: wrap in an explicit cast `int(x)`, or (literal case) change the
  literal to an integral one. Offer the cast as preferred; changing a literal
  silently changes the VALUE and must not be preferred.
- `SignChange` (`uint u = -1;`) — fix: change the declared type to a signed
  one, or cast. Neither is preferred; both change meaning.
- `SignedUnsignedMismatch` (relational compare across signedness) — fix:
  insert a cast on one side. Offer BOTH sides as separate actions; the correct
  one depends on intent.
- `UnreachableCode` — fix: delete the dead tail. Span is already known.
- `MissingReturn { function_name }` — fix: insert `return <default>;` at the
  end of the function body, using a return-type default table
  (`int`->`0`, `float`->`0.0f`? confirm AngelScript literal form,
  `bool`->`false`, `string`->`""`, handle `T@`->`null`, `void`-> no-op /
  no action). Non-preferred: a plausible-looking wrong return is worse than a
  compile error, so this must be clearly labelled and never auto-applied by
  fixAll.

## Design decision (coordinator, 2026-08-30)
`MissingReturn` and the literal-rewriting variant of `FloatTruncation` are
EXCLUDED from `source.fixAll.openplanet`. Both can silently change program
behaviour rather than merely tidy it. They remain available as explicit
per-diagnostic quick fixes. `UnreachableCode` deletion is likewise excluded
from fixAll — deleting code on save is not a tidy-up.

fixAll includes only: the cast insertions (`FloatTruncation` cast variant,
`SignedUnsignedMismatch`), which are behaviour-preserving relative to what the
game compiler already does implicitly.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red, one per family: fixture with the diagnostic -> the expected action(s)
offered, applied edit yields the expected text.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):

`FloatTruncation`:
- literal and non-literal variants both fixable, with DIFFERENT action sets
- targets `int`, `uint`, `int8/16/64` and the unsigned widths — all offered
- targets `float`/`double` -> no diagnostic, no action (regression guard,
  the checker already special-cases these)
- initializer is a complex expression -> cast wraps the WHOLE expression, with
  parens, and the result still parses

`SignChange`:
- `uint u = -1;` fixable; runtime `int`->`uint` of a variable -> no diagnostic
  and no action (game behaviour, guard it)
- all unsigned widths

`SignedUnsignedMismatch`:
- each of `<`, `<=`, `>`, `>=` produces the fix
- BOTH cast-left and cast-right actions offered and distinguishable by title
- `==`/`!=` -> no diagnostic (confirm current behaviour first, then guard it)
- Error / unknown / non-primitive operands -> no action

`UnreachableCode`:
- dead tail after `return`, after `break`, after `continue`, after `throw` if
  supported
- dead tail spanning multiple statements -> single deletion of the whole run
- dead tail containing a nested block / closing braces -> deletion leaves the
  function parseable (assert the result re-parses clean, not just the text)

`MissingReturn`:
- each primitive return type gets the right default
- handle return `T@` -> `null`
- `void` -> no action offered at all
- a function with SOME return paths -> insertion goes at the missing path, or
  the action is not offered if the correct insertion point is ambiguous
  (decide and document; do not guess an insertion point)
- fixAll on a document containing all five families applies ONLY the casts

